  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<para>In this unit, remember to double click on mathematical expressions/equations to enlarge, if needed.</para>
<para>Finding the factors of a number, monomial, or polynomial is an important step used in the activities of this lesson.</para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Factor \(56\) into prime numbers to find all of its factors. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(7 \cdot 2\cdot 2\cdot 2\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Find all of the factors of \(42m^3y^2\).</para>
      
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(7\cdot 2\cdot 3\cdot m\cdot m\cdot m\cdot y\cdot y\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Find all of the common factors between \(6n^2p^2\) and \(9ns^2\). </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(3\) and \(n\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Find the least common multiple (LCM) of \(3x^2\) and \(8x^4\). </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(24x^4\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
